{"kind": "journal", "text": "Approach: map broad anomaly families first (batch 1), then drill into whichever shows a wide robust plateau rather than a single spike. Findings so far: (a) cross sectional transforms cs_rank and cs_z are idempotent at top level since the portfolio is already rank weighted, so they only matter inside composites; (b) short horizon reversal is the dominant effect, SR about 1.0 to 1.2 raw; (c) two orthogonal improvements each add roughly 0.5 SR - time series z scoring the feature over 120 days, and dividing the centered signal by trailing return volatility, which scales position size by risk; (d) range position minr beats raw returns as the reversal carrier, and multi horizon blends of minr beat any single horizon. The vol scaled range reversal family sits on a broad plateau near SR 1.9 to 2.3 across inner lookbacks 10 to 40, outer windows 60 to 250 and vol windows 20 to 120, which is the signature of a real effect rather than a fitted spike. Selection rule for the final three: prefer plateau centers over argmax, and require the neighbours of any chosen point to also score well.", "ts": "2026-09-01T06:49:39.460918+00:00"}
