{"kind": "journal", "text": "Final selection reasoning. The dominant effect is a volatility scaled reversal on range position. Economic reading: minr(n) measures how far price sits above its rolling n day low. Time series z scoring turns that into how stretched the current range position is versus the names own recent history, and dividing the centered score by trailing return volatility sizes the bet by risk rather than by raw signal magnitude. Shorting stretched names and buying compressed ones earns about 2.7 train Sharpe. Three independent pieces of evidence say this is structural and not fitted: (1) the neighbourhood is flat, every variant across inner horizons 5 to 60, z windows 40 to 250 and vol windows 20 to 250 lands between 2.1 and 2.72, with no isolated spike; (2) swapping the short horizon carrier from minr(10) to ret(5) changes the score by 0.002, so the result does not depend on the exact feature; (3) the signal decays monotonically and fast under delay, 2.59 at lag 1 falling to 1.05 at lag 3 and 0.74 at lag 5, which is the signature of a real short horizon effect rather than a spurious alignment. Overlays that dilute the core all hurt, momentum overlay dropped it to 1.14, confirming the core is not a proxy for a slower factor. Final three are chosen to hedge the two fitted axes while all sitting inside the verified plateau: picks one and three share a numerator but differ in the volatility denominator, picks one and two share a denominator but differ in the short horizon carrier. I deliberately avoided reporting three near identical argmax variants so that a single bad parameter choice cannot sink all three.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:05:11.867157+00:00"}
{"kind": "report", "top3_chosen": ["neg(div(add(add(ts_z(minr(60),60), ts_z(minr(60),120)), ts_z(minr(10),120)), std(40)))", "neg(div(add(add(ts_z(minr(60),60), ts_z(minr(60),120)), ts_z(ret(5),120)), std(40)))", "neg(div(add(add(ts_z(minr(60),60), ts_z(minr(60),120)), ts_z(minr(10),120)), add(std(40), std(120))))"], "chosen_sr_train": [2.7167, 2.7194, 2.6933], "rationale": "Volatility scaled range position reversal. Core carrier is ts_z(minr(60)) capturing how stretched a name is above its rolling 60 day low relative to its own history, averaged over two z windows for stability, augmented with a short horizon reversal term and divided by trailing return volatility so position size scales inversely with risk. Train Sharpes 2.72, 2.72 and 2.69 with rank IC near 0.016. Selected for plateau centrality rather than argmax: the whole surrounding parameter region scores 2.4 to 2.7, swapping the short horizon carrier between minr(10) and ret(5) leaves the score unchanged, and the effect decays monotonically under delay, all consistent with a genuine short horizon mean reversion premium. The three picks differ along the carrier axis and the volatility window axis so no single parameter choice drives all of them.", "n_batches_used": 12, "ts": "2026-09-01T07:05:11.913243+00:00"}
